This study systematically investigated the effectiveness of TFSP in improving the fatigue performance of fillet-welded T-joints fabricated from SM490A structural steel. Detailed experiments, including surface geometry measurements, microstructural characterization, Vickers hardness testing, residual stress measurements, and four-point bending fatigue tests, complemented by finite element method (FEM) analyses, were conducted. The main conclusions drawn from this research are as follows: Surface geometry improvement: TFSP significantly improved the weld toe geometry by producing smoother surfaces and enlarged weld toe radii, thereby substantially reducing geometric irregularities and local stress concentrations at weld toes. This optimized surface geometry plays a critical role in delaying fatigue crack initiation. Microstructural refinement and hardness enhancement: TFSP effectively induced substantial grain refinement in the SZ, resulting in the formation of fine, equiaxed ferritic grains. Consequently, Vickers hardness values in the SZ increased significantly, reaching approximately 336 Hv, representing an approximately 54.8% increase relative to untreated weld metal regions (217 Hv). This localized strengthening contributes directly to enhanced fatigue resistance. Residual stress influence: Although TFSP treatment introduced increased longitudinal residual stresses due to severe plastic deformation and thermal effects, minimal changes were observed in transverse residual stresses, which are more critical for fatigue crack initiation. Thus, fatigue performance improvements were predominantly attributed to geometric and microstructural enhancements rather than significant modifications of transverse residual stresses. Fatigue life improvement: Four-point bending fatigue experiments clearly demonstrated that TFSP significantly increased fatigue life compared to untreated (as-welded, AW) specimens, with fatigue life improvements exceeding 100% in several cases. FEM a